  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 320 AM CDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nWidespread showers and embedded thunderstorms will continue to \nslowly drift southward through the area Wednesday. Ample cloud cover \nand rain-cooled air should help limit much reinvigoration of the \nsurface based convection, at least along and north of I-70. The \nleading shortwave exits to the east with a lull in precipitation \ncoverage and intensity, though isolated to scattered showers cannot \nbe ruled out along the stalled boundary. \n\nA second shortwave ejects eastward from the broad upper low and \nproduces yet another round of thunderstorms over eastern KS into \nwestern MO late Wednesday. This moves almost due west to east \nWednesday evening into Wednesday night as the cooler air \nreinforces the boundary slightly southward. The upside is that \nthe position of the boundary is slightly south of the highest \nrain potential from Tuesday night, running roughly along and \nsouth of I-70. Along with the subtle southward shift in the \nsurface boundary and the quick movement of the shortwave within \nthe strong 500mb flow, heavy rain should not sit over one \nparticular area very long, though bouts of heavy rain will \noccasionally pass through with embedded thunderstorms. Rainfall \nand cloud cover will keep temperatures 10-15 degrees cooler than\nthe record-level territory achieved Monday and Tuesday. That \nsaid, it's still impressive to be in mid-October and reach the \nlow-80s with cloud cover and rainfall.\n\nLong range guidance (ECM/GFS) remain far departed in the handling of \nthe upper level pattern Thursday into Friday. However, the dominate \nsurface feature is a strong surface high that builds into the Plains \nas a separate long wave trough drops southeast through the \nGreat Lakes. The two, in tandem, will help clear the frontal \nboundary to the south of the area, bringing cooler, drier air to\nthe region. The center of the ridge and eastward movement \nvaries slightly between guidance. That said, it does look to be \npositioned overhead Saturday morning, and possibly into Sunday \nmorning. Clear skies and light surface wind could bring the \ncoolest temperatures of the season dipping below normal, for a \nchange. LREF probabilistic (25th/75th percentiles) guidance \nshows less spread (5-7 degrees) in Saturday morning lows, \nranging from the mid-30s to low-40s. Sunday morning's spread \nincreases slightly with the ECM pulling the surface high \neastward a little quicker, though Sunday morning lows range from\nthe mid-30s to mid-40s between the IQR. If narrows in on this \nsolution, it could be the first decent chance at frost this \nfall.",
